 it's pretty bad um but i think  we presume there'd been some sort of  nuclear explosion uh something  in that in that vein i mean it would  have been interesting if it had been a  worldwide pandemic and then then we'd  have  you know no they would think we started  it felicity  exactly the biggest publicity stunt of  all time  oh actually for both of you uh i'm uh  curious what is it like  watching a movie like this for the first  time because you weren't on set  for half the movie so there has to be  like a reaction for you that you really  don't know  what how it was filmed or what people  were doing on the day  so for each of you can you talk about  your reaction seeing the finished film  for me it was almost like i was only in  a quarter of the movie  because yes you have the george clooney  in the  with the little girl in iceland and all  that craziness but  for us so much of the film  um or our section of the film was built  after we had shot our stuff in terms of  vfx  obviously we're shooting in space and so  therefore space  has to now be inputted into the the film  even though  we were interacting with that  environment obviously not being in that  environment  so you know there are occasionally these  films where  you are just as awed by watching the  film as the audience  around you you know i i watched it with  my wife and she looked at my expression  now and again it was almost as if i  hadn't been there when we were shooting  the film  because it was just so awe-inspiring i  was so impressed by the fact that george  was in that  unbelievable environment um while  directing and acting and just being so  cold  but then just so impressive what uh the  the visual effects of the film were  after we had shot our stuff as well  yeah it was um it was actually yeah it  was  fascinating watching it and just how  beautifully constructed it was and how  artful it is  and you know that opening sequence with  the blood uh  running around as as george's is um  doing the the chemotherapy i really was  totally  struck by that imagery because you never  quite know how a film is gonna  turn out but um but just and exploring  such  personal themes and felt i mean it's  incredibly  moving and paxa packs a huge punch by  the end of the film  but it was i mean particularly watching  it now in the in the moment that we find  ourselves in it's  just yeah you're watching it looking for  hope and  looking for answers to this situation  that we find ourselves in

